super mario 64 sunshine and galaxy are  finally on nintendo switch in glorious  hd  but you might be wondering how some of  these games will control on the switch  how will flood work in sunshine without  the gamecube's analog triggers and how  will you play galaxy without motion  controls  well in this video we'll be breaking  down each game and talking about how  they've been adapted to play on nintendo  switch  and let me tell you the developers did a  fantastic job transitioning the controls  let's start with mario 64. this is  probably the least different of the  bunch but there are still some changes  worth mentioning first  you can use the right stick to move the  camera but it still clicks to fix  positions like you're hitting one of the  c buttons on the n64 controller  so you can't freely move it around in  full 360 degrees  this isn't exactly new as the virtual  console versions of mario 64 also did  this  but it's something to note if you  haven't played the game since the n64  or are playing it for the first time the  l r buttons still let you change the  camera mode from closer to farther away  the zl and zr buttons both act as the z  button from the n64 which allows you to  duck  crouch and perform other moves like the  long jump and ground pound  this is also true for jumping punching  kicking and swimming  now you can use a or b to jump and swim  on n64 those are both assigned to the a  button  you can now also use x or y to punch and  kick whereas these were done with the b  button on the n64  otherwise the controls are all the same  moving on to mario sunshine the most  notable change is how flood operates  the gamecube had what were called analog  triggers which essentially meant you  could push them lightly or down fully  for a different effect  in super mario sunshine you hit the r  button lightly to shoot water while  running and you press it all the way  down to stand still so you can aim and  spray  what nintendo did for the switch version  was take advantage of the r and zr  buttons  as you can see on the control screen  pressing z r gives you a running squirt  and pressing r allows you to stop and  squirt  if you do decide to stop and squirt  you'll need to press the right stick in  to get into mario cam which was y on the  gamecube controller  the guidebook was moved from the z  button on the gamecube controller to the  l button on the switch controller and zl  has become  center camera which was l on the  gamecube controller x is still used to  switch nozzles  and the gamecube style x button is still  shown on screen which is nice for fans  and keeping authenticity but  could be confusing for new players  speaking of changing button icons the b  button on the gamecube controller used  to be how you'd interact with npcs  but now you can use a or b and while the  game retains the red circle above  character's head since the  b button was a red circle on the  gamecube controller the letter inside  has  changed from b to a either will do the  trick despite it just saying a  actions that used to be assigned to b  like picking up items throwing items and  body slamming have been moved to the y  button  on the switch a and b are now just for  jumping swimming and talking  one final thing to note is that while  the gamecube controller could be plugged  into the switch through the adapter  release for super smash bros  sunshine will not take advantage of the  gamecube controller's functionality and  will instead act like a default usb  controller so what happens is the z  button on the gamecube controller  becomes what was zr  on the switch controller and the r  button on gamecube stays r  on the switch so you can stop and squirt  and do a running squirt but the l button  just opens the guidebook so you can't  center the camera which isn't a huge  deal  so it's definitely possible to play  sunshine on twitch with a gamecube  controller but all the buttons are  reassigned to mimic the switch  controller so it's not one-to-one with  the original  last but not least is super mario galaxy  which arguably has the most changes  since it was originally a wii game that  relied on motion controls  are you forced to use the joy-con  separated from mario galaxy and if so  how does it work on the switch light  well fear not because the button  controls are real  and they're spectacular so first let's  talk about how it works if you're  playing with a pro controller or  joy-cons on a grip  the gyro on both the pro controller and  joy cons work as the pointer  you still use that to select stuff on  the menu and collect star bits like with  the wii remote but  no sensor bar required this time if you  ever want to recenter the gyro all you  need to do is hit the r button  the biggest change in my opinion is that  you no longer need to shake the  controller to spin  which is a core mechanic to the game you  can now just press y or x  you can still shake the right joy con or  pro controller if you want but you don't  have to  jumping and swimming are now on both a  and b and while shooting star bits used  to be the b button on the wii remote now  it's on zr  on the switch zl now allows you to  crouch which was z  in the wii version and l resets the  camera which was c on the wii nunchuck  lastly before you can move the camera  with the d-pad on the wiimote but now  you can use the right stick  since it was originally on the d-pad it  isn't clicks just like mario 64 was and  despite having the right stick now it's  no different here  the co-star mode for mario galaxy also  makes a return where the game becomes  multiplayer and the second player is  only able to shoot star bits and stop  enemies it can be played with two  horizontal joy-cons two pro controllers  or any combination really it's not  limited to just two horizontal joy-cons  so now let's move on to handheld mode  which is also how the switch light will  operate  it's pretty much all the same except no  gyro functionality  instead you have to touch the screen to  select things and collect star bits  it's not bad but you have to move your  hand off the right joy con to do it  so you can't be jumping and collecting  star bits at the same time which you  can do with the joy cons of the pro  controller definitely play with the  controller if you can but handheld isn't  bad just less ideal  lastly and this applies to all three  games the select button is used to  suspend the game  check controls and actions and return to  the title selection if you want to  choose a different game  and that's it nintendo did a fantastic  job adapting each of these games to work  with switch controls and it's certainly  nice to have all these games in hd 2.  this gets me excited for the possibility  of how other wii games could come to  switch  skyward sword anyone if you have any  further questions about how the controls  work do not hesitate to comment down  below and i'll answer them as best as i  can  for more coverage on super mario 3d  all-stars including our review stay  tuned to gamespot  [Music]  you
 

